course: history
lesson: 33
title: The First World War
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: By 1914 the big countries of Europe were rivals with large armies, tied together in two groups of allies, so a quarrel between two of them could pull in all the rest, like a playground fight where every friend joins in.
explain: The spark came in June 1914, when the heir to Austria-Hungary's throne, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, was shot in Sarajevo, and within weeks most of Europe was at war.
explain: Many soldiers thought it would be over by Christmas, but on the Western Front both sides dug long muddy trenches and neither could break through for years.
explain: Yet at Christmas 1914 in some places soldiers from both sides climbed out, sang carols, swapped gifts and even played football, because ordinary soldiers did not hate each other.
explain: The fighting stopped at 11 o'clock on 11 November 1918, and today people wear red poppies to remember everyone who died.
fact: By 1914 Europe's big powers were rivals in two alliances: Germany and Austria-Hungary on one side, and France, Russia and Britain on the other.
fact: In June 1914 Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the throne of Austria-Hungary, was shot in Sarajevo, and the alliances pulled one country after another into war.
fact: The First World War lasted from 1914 to 1918.
fact: Many soldiers believed the war would be over by Christmas 1914, but it lasted more than four years.
fact: On the Western Front armies dug long lines of trenches facing each other, muddy, wet and full of rats, and neither side could break through for years.
fact: At Christmas 1914 in some places soldiers on both sides stopped fighting, sang carols, swapped gifts and played football in no man's land, the ground between the trenches.
fact: The war brought new machines such as tanks, aeroplanes, submarines and machine guns, which made it far deadlier than earlier wars.
fact: Soldiers came from many lands, including India, Africa, Canada, Australia and New Zealand, and the landing at Gallipoli in 1915 is remembered on Anzac Day.
fact: Ukrainian lands were split between Russia and Austria-Hungary, so Ukrainians fought in both the Russian and the Austro-Hungarian armies, sometimes on opposite sides of the same front.
fact: At home people faced food shortages, and women did many jobs of the men who had gone to fight.
fact: The fighting stopped at 11 o'clock on the 11th day of the 11th month of 1918, and about 20 million people had died.
fact: After the war old empires collapsed and new countries appeared on the map, and people hoped it had been the war to end all wars.
fact: A Canadian doctor, John McCrae, wrote the poem In Flanders Fields about the red poppies that grew on the battlefields, and people wear poppies in November to remember those who died in wars.
exercise: Which countries were on the same side as Germany in 1914? | Austria-Hungary | France and Britain | Russia and France | Canada and Australia
exercise: What was the spark that started the war? | The shooting of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo | The sinking of the Spanish Armada off the coast of England | A football match | The fall of Constantinople
exercise: In which years was the First World War fought? | 1914 to 1918 | 1939 to 1945 | 1337 to 1453 | 1861 to 1865
exercise: What did many soldiers believe at the start? | The war would be over by Christmas | The war would last a hundred years | There would be no fighting | They would fight on the Moon
exercise: What were the trenches like? | Muddy, wet and full of rats | Warm and comfortable, with beds and stoves | Made of gold | Built high in the trees
exercise: What happened in the Christmas truce of 1914? | Soldiers on both sides sang carols and swapped gifts | The war ended for ever, and everyone went home | Everyone went home for a month to see their families | Santa visited the trenches
exercise: What was no man's land? | The ground between the two lines of trenches | A country with no people in it at all, far to the east | An island in the Pacific | A king's private garden
exercise: Which new machine of war first appeared in this war? | The tank | The longbow | The catapult | The trebuchet
exercise: Where did soldiers come from to fight? | From many lands, including India, Africa, Canada and Australia | Only from Germany and France, nowhere else in the world at all | Only from Britain | Only from the USA
exercise: Which day remembers the landing at Gallipoli? | Anzac Day | Christmas Day | Independence Day | Bastille Day
exercise: In which armies did Ukrainians fight in this war? | Both the Russian and the Austro-Hungarian armies | Only the British army | None, Ukraine was far from the war and never saw any fighting | Only the American army
exercise: What changed for women at home during the war? | They did many jobs of the men who had gone | They were not allowed to work anywhere outside the home | They all became soldiers | Nothing changed for anyone
exercise: When did the fighting stop? | At 11 o'clock on 11 November 1918 | At midnight on 1 January 1915, with fireworks | On Christmas Day 1914 | In summer 1945
exercise: About how many people died in the First World War? | About 20 million | About 20 thousand | About 2 billion people | About 200 soldiers
exercise: Why do people wear poppies in November? | To remember those who died in wars | To celebrate the harvest | To show they like gardening and growing flowers | For Halloween
exercise: Who wrote the poem In Flanders Fields? | John McCrae, a Canadian doctor | William Shakespeare | Winston Churchill, the prime minister | Martin Luther
test: cousin says ww1 kicked off cos one guy got shot. that it? | That was the spark, but rival alliances pulled everyone in | Yes, nothing else mattered at all, and nobody else wanted war | No, it started over football | No, it started in 1939
test: If a war will be over by Christmas, why did the soldiers of 1914 find that so wrong? | It lasted more than four years | It lasted only a week | It ended before it really began | It happened in one summer, from June to August
test: Why could neither side break through on the Western Front for years? | Both sides were dug into strong trenches with deadly new weapons | They were not allowed to move | Nobody wanted to win | The trenches were filled with water every day
test: Two soldiers from enemy armies kick a football together at Christmas 1914. What does that tell us? | Ordinary soldiers did not hate each other | They had stopped the war for ever and gone home | They were on the same team | It never happened
test: A cartoon shows the First World War fought only by Britain and Germany. What is missing? | Soldiers from many countries and empires around the world | Nothing, only two countries fought, and only for a few days | Dinosaurs | The Romans
test: What would happen to food at home if ships bringing grain were sunk by submarines? | There would be shortages | There would be much more food | Prices would go down to zero | Nothing would change at all
test: Why might two Ukrainian brothers have ended up on opposite sides in 1914? | Ukraine was split between Russia and Austria-Hungary | They chose by flipping a coin | Ukraine fought against itself on purpose | That could never happen
test: At 11 a.m. on 11 November people in Britain go quiet for two minutes. Why then? | That is the moment the fighting stopped in 1918 | It is lunchtime | It is when the war started in 1914 | It is the king's birthday, when he wakes up
test: Why did people call it the war to end all wars? | They hoped such a terrible war would never happen again | It ended every war in history, and there has never been one since | It was the very first war | It had no fighting
test: grandad wears a red poppy in november. why poppies, why not roses? | Poppies grew on the battlefields of Flanders | Roses were banned | Poppies are cheaper | Because of a football club
exam: Identify the event commonly regarded as the immediate occasion of the First World War. | The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo | The sinking of the Titanic | The invasion of Poland by the armies of Germany and Russia | The fall of Constantinople
exam: Ascertain the precise moment at which hostilities ceased in nineteen hundred and eighteen. | The eleventh hour of the eleventh day of the eleventh month | Midnight upon the first day of January | Noon upon Christmas Day | The sixth hour of the sixth day of June
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Christmas truce of nineteen hundred and fourteen? | Soldiers of opposing armies ceased fighting locally and exchanged gifts | The war was formally concluded | All armies withdrew from France | No such event is recorded
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the wearing of red poppies. | It commemorates those who have died in wars | It celebrates the harvest | It marks the founding of the United Nations | It honours the invention of the tank
